// The collections book, written in Cufet.
//
// This file is bundled into the checker (see TypeChecker.WithPrelude): every program is checked
// and compiled as though it began with these statements. The object below shares its name with
// the native collections book, and the two resolve as ONE module — a member defined here is the
// only implementation of that member, and anything not defined here (today only the matrix TYPE
// itself) is still the native book's, reached through the same pulled name.

/// Working with a **series** and a **matrix** — the operations that are the same every time you
/// write them.
///
/// ★ Everything here hands back a NEW value. Nothing in this book changes what you gave it, so a
/// series can be passed to any of these and still be the series you had.
Define object collections with () and module:
    /// The smallest value, or **void for an empty series**.
    ///
    /// An empty series has no smallest — there is no number to return and no sensible stand-in, so
    /// this says so rather than inventing a zero. `but void is` is how you supply a fallback.
    ///
    /// **The first of equal values is the one kept**, which matters only if you are comparing
    /// records by one field and expecting a particular tie to survive.
    Bind voidable number to minimum, given (the series of number xs):
        If the number of xs is 0, return void.
        Define best as the first of xs.
        For each candidate in xs, repeat:
            If candidate is less than best, best becomes candidate.
        Done.
        Return best.
    Done.

    /// The largest value, or **void for an empty series** — the mirror of `minimum`, with the same
    /// two answers to the same two questions: nothing to return for an empty series, and the first
    /// of equal values is the one kept.
    Bind voidable number to maximum, given (the series of number xs):
        If the number of xs is 0, return void.
        Define best as the first of xs.
        For each candidate in xs, repeat:
            If candidate is greater than best, best becomes candidate.
        Done.
        Return best.
    Done.

    /// The mean, **exactly** — or void for an empty series.
    ///
    /// One running sum and then one division, all in decimal, so there is no float drift to
    /// accumulate: the average of `(0.1, 0.2, 0.3)` is exactly `0.2` and not `0.19999999999999998`.
    /// That is the whole reason to reach for this rather than summing by hand.
    ///
    /// ⚠ It is a MEAN. A series with one wild value has a mean that describes none of it.
    Bind voidable number to average, given (the series of number xs):
        If the number of xs is 0, return void.
        Define sum as 0.
        For each value in xs, repeat:
            The sum becomes sum + value.
        Done.
        Return sum / (the number of xs).
    Done.

    /// Columns become rows.
    ///
    /// A `2 by 3` matrix comes back `3 by 2`, and the original is untouched. Transposing twice
    /// gives you back what you started with.
    Bind matrix to transpose, given (the matrix m):
        Define height as the rows of m.
        Define width as the columns of m.
        Define flipped as a matrix with width by height.
        For each row in range 1 to height, repeat:
            For each column in range 1 to width, repeat:
                The item at (column, row) of flipped becomes the item at (row, column) of m.
            Done.
        Done.
        Return flipped.
    Done.

    /// The same series with **later duplicates dropped**, in the order they first appeared.
    ///
    /// Works on a series of anything `is` can compare, and keeps the element type — a series of
    /// text comes back a series of text. The series you gave is untouched.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **It compares every kept value against every candidate**, so it costs more the longer the
    /// series and the more distinct values it holds. Fine for a list you can read; think twice for
    /// one you cannot.
    Bind series of element to unique, given (the series of element xs):
        Define kept as a series of element.
        For each candidate in xs, repeat:
            Define already as false.
            For each existing in kept, repeat:
                If existing is candidate, already becomes true.
            Done.
            If already is false, insert candidate into kept.
        Done.
        Return kept.
    Done.
Done.
